description:
pkix-lint/src/lib.rs:642:

    pub trait Lint: Send + Sync {
        ...
    }

The trait declaration carries Send + Sync as a supertrait. This means
every implementor MUST be Send + Sync, even if they have no use for
threading. The LintRunner doc (lib.rs:982-985) documents this:

    /// # Thread safety
    ///
    /// `LintRunner` is `Send + Sync` as long as all supplied lints are `Send + Sync`
    /// (enforced by the `Lint: Send + Sync` bound).

But the Lint trait's own doc (lib.rs:629-642) says only:

    /// # Object safety
    ///
    /// The trait is object-safe: `Box<dyn Lint>` and `&dyn Lint` both work.

The Send + Sync requirement is in the syntax but not in the prose. A
caller implementing a Lint that wraps `Rc<RefCell<State>>` or
`MaybeUninit<...>` gets a confusing 'Send is not implemented for X'
compile error from `LintRunner::new(vec![Box::new(MyLint)])` rather
than a clear contract statement at trait definition.

# Suggested fix

Add to the Lint trait rustdoc:

    /// # Thread safety
    ///
    /// `Lint: Send + Sync` is a hard requirement so that `LintRunner`
    /// (which stores `Vec<Box<dyn Lint>>`) is itself `Send + Sync`. This
    /// is enforced by the supertrait bound. Implementors using
    /// non-thread-safe interior state (`Rc`, `RefCell`, `Cell`) must
    /// switch to `Arc`/`Mutex` or a synchronization primitive that
    /// implements `Send + Sync`.

This makes the contract discoverable at the right level (where the
caller reads the trait) rather than deferred to LintRunner.

# Why P3

The compiler enforces the rule even without the prose. The harm is
caller experience (one round of compilation diagnostics) rather than
silent corruption. But it's a one-line rustdoc fix.
